/* CODIVIA platform — Coding Guidelines reference content */
const CODING_GUIDE = {"icd":{"label":"ICD-10-CM","sub":"Official Guidelines · Section I.C","color":"#3B82F6","chapters":[{"n":1,"t":"Certain Infectious and Parasitic Diseases","r":"A00-B99","ref":"I.C.1","i":"Chapter 1 classifies communicable and parasitic conditions. Its guidelines deal mainly with how to sequence an infection against its manifestations, and when to add a separate organism code.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Infections","b":"Code assignment turns on whether the provider has documented HIV disease as opposed to a positive test result alone. Once a patient has been assigned the HIV disease code, that assignment applies to every subsequent encounter.","p":["B20 is assigned only when the provider documents HIV disease or an HIV-related illness.","Asymptomatic HIV positive status with no documented HIV-related condition is Z21.","Once B20 has been assigned for a patient, never revert to Z21 on a later encounter.","If the reason for admission is an HIV-related condition, sequence B20 first, then the related conditions.","If the reason for admission is unrelated to HIV, sequence the unrelated condition first and B20 as secondary.","Inconclusive serology without a diagnosis is R75."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Infectious Agents as the Cause of Diseases Classified Elsewhere","b":"Some codes identify a condition without naming the organism responsible. A secondary code from the B95-B97 range supplies that detail.","p":["Use B95-B97 as an additional code when the condition code does not identify the organism.","These codes are never sequenced as the principal or first-listed diagnosis."]},{"ref":"c","t":"Infections Resistant to Antibiotics","b":"Resistance is reported separately from the infection itself, and only when the provider documents it.","p":["Assign a code from Z16 as an additional code when the provider documents resistance.","Sequence the infection code first, then the resistance code."]},{"ref":"d","t":"Sepsis, Severe Sepsis and Septic Shock","b":"This subsection generates more sequencing errors than any other in the chapter. The distinction between sepsis, severe sepsis and septic shock decides how many codes are needed and in what order.","p":["Sepsis alone: assign the code for the underlying systemic infection.","Severe sepsis requires a minimum of two codes: the underlying infection, then a code from R65.2-.","Severe sepsis also requires additional codes for each associated acute organ dysfunction.","Septic shock is coded R65.21 and is never assigned as a principal diagnosis.","For septic shock, the systemic infection is sequenced first, then R65.21, then the organ dysfunction.","If severe sepsis develops after admission, the underlying infection is still sequenced first but severe sepsis is not the principal diagnosis.","Sepsis due to a postprocedural infection requires the complication code first."]}]},{"n":2,"t":"Neoplasms","r":"C00-D49","ref":"I.C.2","i":"Chapter 2 guidelines govern how to find the right neoplasm code and, more importantly, how to sequence it against the treatment being delivered.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Treatment Directed at the Malignancy","b":"The site being treated determines the first-listed code. This is the rule most often applied incorrectly.","p":["When treatment is directed at the primary site, sequence the primary malignancy first.","When treatment is directed at a secondary site, sequence the secondary site first and the primary second.","Look up the histologic type in the Alphabetic Index first — it directs you to the correct Neoplasm Table column."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Encounters for Chemotherapy, Immunotherapy and Radiation","b":"When the entire encounter exists to deliver anti-neoplastic therapy, a Z code takes first position regardless of the malignancy.","p":["Sequence Z51.0 first for radiation therapy, Z51.11 for chemotherapy, Z51.12 for immunotherapy.","The malignancy is coded as a secondary diagnosis.","If more than one modality is delivered in the same encounter, report each Z code."]},{"ref":"c","t":"Primary Malignancy Previously Excised","b":"A malignancy that no longer exists is history, not a current condition.","p":["When a primary site has been excised and no further treatment is directed to it, use a Z85 personal history code.","Do not continue reporting the malignancy code once the site has been eradicated.","If there is any remaining malignancy or ongoing treatment, the malignancy code still applies."]},{"ref":"d","t":"Complications Associated with Neoplasms","b":"Anaemia, dehydration and pain frequently accompany malignancy, and each has its own sequencing rule.","p":["Anaemia associated with a malignancy: sequence the neoplasm first, then D63.0.","Anaemia due to antineoplastic chemotherapy: sequence the anaemia first, then the neoplasm, then the adverse effect code.","When the encounter is for management of dehydration only, sequence the dehydration first.","Pain associated with a neoplasm is G89.3 when pain control is the reason for the encounter."]}]},{"n":3,"t":"Diseases of the Blood and Blood-Forming Organs","r":"D50-D89","ref":"I.C.3","i":"Chapter 3 covers anaemias and immune mechanism disorders. The guidelines focus on linking an anaemia to its underlying cause and on the sequencing that follows.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Anaemia Associated with an Underlying Condition","b":"Most anaemia coding errors come from reporting the anaemia alone when a cause is documented.","p":["Anaemia in chronic kidney disease is coded D63.1, with the CKD stage sequenced first.","Anaemia in neoplastic disease is D63.0, with the neoplasm sequenced first.","Anaemia due to antineoplastic chemotherapy is D64.81 — sequence the anaemia first when it is the reason for the encounter.","Aplastic anaemia due to a drug requires the adverse effect code from the Table of Drugs and Chemicals."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Sickle-Cell Disease","b":"Sickle-cell codes are combination codes and the crisis is never reported separately.","p":["Sickle-cell disease with crisis is a single combination code.","Do not assign an additional code for the crisis.","Sickle-cell trait is a carrier state, not a disease, and is coded D57.3."]}]},{"n":4,"t":"Endocrine, Nutritional and Metabolic Diseases","r":"E00-E89","ref":"I.C.4","i":"Chapter 4 is dominated by diabetes mellitus, which is the single most heavily tested topic in the code set. The combination-code structure and the 'with' convention decide almost every answer.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Diabetes Mellitus and the Use of Insulin","b":"Diabetes codes are combination codes carrying the type, the body system affected and the complication in one code. Report as many as needed to describe every documented complication.","p":["The type of diabetes must be documented. If the type is not stated, default to type 2 (E11).","Assign as many diabetes codes as necessary to describe all complications the patient has.","Sequence them according to the reason for the encounter.","Do not assume a patient is type 1 simply because insulin is used.","Add Z79.4 for long-term insulin use, or Z79.84 for long-term oral hypoglycaemic drugs.","Do not assign Z79.4 to a type 1 patient — insulin use is inherent to the type."]},{"ref":"a.1","t":"Diabetes with Associated Conditions — the 'with' convention","b":"The word 'with' in the Alphabetic Index creates an assumed causal relationship. This convention is the source of most diabetes coding disputes.","p":["'With' in the Index means the two conditions are assumed to be related, with no provider link required.","The assumption applies whether the sub-terms appear under 'with' in the Index or in a tabular instructional note.","The assumption does not apply if the provider documents that the conditions are unrelated.","E11.22 covers type 2 diabetes with diabetic chronic kidney disease — add the N18 code for the CKD stage.","Diabetic retinopathy codes require the type of retinopathy and whether macular oedema is present."]},{"ref":"a.2","t":"Secondary Diabetes Mellitus","b":"Diabetes caused by another condition, a drug, or a procedure is classified separately from type 1 and type 2.","p":["E08 — diabetes due to an underlying condition. Sequence the underlying condition first.","E09 — drug or chemical induced diabetes. Sequence E09 first, then the adverse effect or poisoning code.","E13 — other specified diabetes, including postprocedural and postpancreatectomy diabetes.","For postpancreatectomy diabetes, also assign the acquired absence of pancreas code."]},{"ref":"a.3","t":"Complications Due to Insulin Pump Malfunction","b":"Pump failures are coded as mechanical complications, and the sequencing depends on whether the failure caused underdose or overdose.","p":["Underdose from a pump malfunction: sequence the mechanical complication first, then the diabetes code, then the underdosing code.","Overdose from a pump malfunction: sequence the mechanical complication first, then the poisoning code."]}]},{"n":5,"t":"Mental, Behavioural and Neurodevelopmental Disorders","r":"F01-F99","ref":"I.C.5","i":"The guidelines here centre on the substance use hierarchy, which determines that only one code per substance is ever reported.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Substance Use, Abuse and Dependence","b":"When more than one level of use is documented for the same substance, a strict hierarchy applies and only the highest level is coded.","p":["If use, abuse and dependence are all documented for one substance, assign only the dependence code.","If both abuse and dependence are documented, assign only the dependence code.","If both use and abuse are documented, assign only the abuse code.","The hierarchy applies per substance — a patient may have dependence on one substance and abuse of another.","'In remission' requires provider documentation and is never inferred by the coder."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Blood Alcohol Level and Psychoactive Substance Use","b":"A documented blood alcohol level is reported additionally.","p":["Assign a code from Y90 when the provider documents a blood alcohol level.","Codes for psychoactive substance use (F10.9-, F11.9-) are only assigned when the provider links the use to a health condition."]}]},{"n":6,"t":"Diseases of the Nervous System","r":"G00-G99","ref":"I.C.6","i":"Chapter 6 guidelines are mostly about pain. The G89 category has sequencing rules that depend entirely on why the patient presented.","s":[{"ref":"b","t":"Pain — Category G89","b":"Whether G89 is sequenced first depends on the purpose of the encounter, not on the severity of the pain.","p":["Do not assign G89 if the underlying diagnosis is known and the encounter is to treat that condition.","When the encounter is specifically for PAIN CONTROL or pain management, sequence the G89 code first.","When the encounter is to treat the underlying condition, sequence the condition first and G89 as additional.","G89 codes may be used with codes from other chapters to provide more detail about acute or chronic pain.","There is no time frame defining when pain becomes chronic — rely on provider documentation."]},{"ref":"b.1","t":"Postoperative Pain","b":"Routine post-surgical pain is expected and not coded. Only pain beyond the expected course is reported.","p":["Routine or expected postoperative pain immediately after surgery is not coded.","Postoperative pain not associated with a specific complication is coded to G89.18 or G89.28.","Postoperative pain associated with a specific complication is coded to the complication chapter.","The default for postoperative pain documented without further specification is acute."]},{"ref":"b.2","t":"Neoplasm Related Pain","b":"Pain documented as related to a malignancy has its own code, used regardless of whether the pain is acute or chronic.","p":["Assign G89.3 for pain documented as related to, associated with or due to cancer.","When the encounter is for pain control, sequence G89.3 first and the malignancy as additional."]}]},{"n":7,"t":"Diseases of the Eye and Adnexa","r":"H00-H59","ref":"I.C.7","i":"Chapter 7 guidelines are dominated by glaucoma staging and the near-universal requirement for laterality.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Glaucoma","b":"Glaucoma codes carry both the type and a stage character, and the rules change when the two eyes differ.","p":["Assign the code for the type of glaucoma and add the 7th character for the stage.","Stages are: unspecified, mild, moderate, severe, and indeterminate.","If both eyes have the SAME type and SAME stage, assign one bilateral code with that stage.","If each eye has a DIFFERENT type or a DIFFERENT stage, assign separate codes for each eye.","If the stage changes during an admission, assign the code for the highest stage documented.","Indeterminate stage means the stage could not be determined clinically — it is not the same as unspecified."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Laterality and Blindness","b":"Nearly every code in this chapter requires a side, and blindness codes require a category per eye.","p":["Assign right, left or bilateral wherever the code set offers it.","Unspecified laterality should be rare and usually signals a query is needed.","Blindness and low vision codes require the visual impairment category for each eye."]}]},{"n":8,"t":"Diseases of the Ear and Mastoid Process","r":"H60-H95","ref":"I.C.8","i":"A short chapter. Laterality and the classification of hearing loss by mechanism are the recurring requirements.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"General Requirements","b":"Documentation must support both the side and the specific type of condition.","p":["Most codes require right, left or bilateral.","Otitis media is classified by acuity and by whether it is suppurative or non-suppurative.","Hearing loss is classified as conductive, sensorineural or mixed, and by ear.","Where hearing loss differs between ears, report the code describing the combination."]}]},{"n":9,"t":"Diseases of the Circulatory System","r":"I00-I99","ref":"I.C.9","i":"Chapter 9 is the most rule-dense chapter in the code set. Hypertension combination codes and myocardial infarction timing rules generate the majority of errors.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Hypertension","b":"ICD-10-CM classifies hypertension as a single code unless heart or kidney disease is also present, in which case combination codes apply.","p":["Essential hypertension is I10. There is no longer a benign or malignant distinction.","Hypertension with heart disease uses I11 only when the provider documents a causal link, or a 'with' convention applies.","If the provider states the heart condition is not due to hypertension, code them separately.","Hypertensive chronic kidney disease (I12) is ALWAYS assumed related — no provider link is needed.","Hypertensive heart and chronic kidney disease (I13) applies when both are present.","For I11 and I13, add the heart failure code to identify the type of failure.","For I12 and I13, add the N18 code to identify the CKD stage."]},{"ref":"a.1","t":"Hypertensive Crisis","b":"Crisis states are reported alongside the underlying hypertension, never instead of it.","p":["Assign a code from I16 for documented hypertensive urgency, crisis or emergency.","Sequence the I16 code together with the underlying hypertension code.","Sequencing depends on the circumstances of the encounter."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Cerebrovascular Disease and Sequelae","b":"The distinction between an acute cerebrovascular event and its lasting effects governs both code choice and sequencing.","p":["Acute cerebrovascular infarction is coded from I63.","Codes from I60-I67 are not used for a patient with a history of stroke and no residual deficits — use Z86.73.","Category I69 reports the LATE EFFECTS of cerebrovascular disease.","I69 codes may be assigned at any time after the initial event, including during the same encounter as the acute code if deficits are present.","Code any residual deficit even when the stroke itself is historical.","Report deficit codes with the appropriate dominant or non-dominant side."]},{"ref":"c","t":"Acute Myocardial Infarction","b":"The four-week rule defines whether an infarction is still acute, and a second infarction within that window needs an extra code.","p":["An acute MI is coded from I21 for the first four weeks from onset.","After four weeks, if the patient requires continued care, use an aftercare or old MI code (I25.2).","A subsequent MI occurring within four weeks of an initial MI requires a code from I22 in addition to the I21 code.","Sequencing of I21 and I22 depends on the circumstances of the encounter.","STEMI codes identify the site of the infarction. NSTEMI is I21.4.","If a STEMI converts to an NSTEMI due to thrombolytic therapy, it remains coded as STEMI."]},{"ref":"d","t":"Heart Failure","b":"Heart failure codes require both the type and the acuity, and are frequently reported alongside hypertension combination codes.","p":["Specify systolic, diastolic or combined systolic and diastolic failure.","Specify acute, chronic, or acute on chronic.","When heart failure accompanies hypertension, report it in addition to the I11 or I13 code.","Heart failure documented without type or acuity defaults to I50.9."]}]},{"n":10,"t":"Diseases of the Respiratory System","r":"J00-J99","ref":"I.C.10","i":"Chapter 10 guidelines govern how COPD and asthma interact with acute infections and exacerbations, and how ventilator-associated pneumonia is identified.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease and Asthma","b":"The relationship between COPD, asthma and any superimposed infection determines how many codes are needed.","p":["COPD with acute exacerbation is J44.1. An exacerbation is a worsening, not an infection.","COPD with acute lower respiratory infection is J44.0 — code the infection additionally.","If both an infection and an exacerbation are documented, assign both J44.0 and J44.1.","Asthma with COPD: assign J44.- together with the appropriate asthma code from J45.","Asthma codes require severity and status.","Status asthmaticus outranks exacerbation — if both are documented, code status asthmaticus.","Never assign an exacerbation code together with a status asthmaticus code for the same episode."]},{"ref":"d","t":"Ventilator Associated Pneumonia","b":"This code is only assigned when the provider makes the link explicitly.","p":["Assign J95.851 only when the provider documents ventilator-associated pneumonia.","Do not assign it simply because a patient on a ventilator develops pneumonia.","Code the causal organism additionally when identified.","If the patient is admitted with pneumonia and later develops VAP, assign both codes."]}]},{"n":11,"t":"Diseases of the Digestive System","r":"K00-K95","ref":"I.C.11","i":"Chapter 11 guidelines cover ulcer bleeding status, hernia detail, and the classification of postprocedural digestive complications.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Ulcers and Gastrointestinal Bleeding","b":"Ulcer codes combine the site with the presence of haemorrhage or perforation.","p":["Gastric, duodenal and peptic ulcer codes specify with haemorrhage, with perforation, with both, or without either.","Bleeding status must be documented — do not assume it from a low haemoglobin.","Gastrointestinal haemorrhage without a stated source is coded K92.2."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Hernias and Obstruction","b":"Hernia codes carry site, laterality and whether obstruction or gangrene is present.","p":["Specify the site and, where applicable, laterality.","Specify whether obstruction, gangrene, or both are present.","Recurrent hernias have their own codes.","Intestinal obstruction requires the cause and site where documented."]},{"ref":"c","t":"Postprocedural Digestive Complications","b":"Complications are only coded when the provider documents the causal relationship.","p":["Assign K91.- codes for complications of digestive system procedures.","Add a code for the specific condition where one exists.","Do not assume a complication from the presence of a finding after surgery."]}]},{"n":12,"t":"Diseases of the Skin and Subcutaneous Tissue","r":"L00-L99","ref":"I.C.12","i":"Chapter 12 guidelines are dominated by ulcer coding. Pressure ulcers and non-pressure chronic ulcers follow different rules and are frequently confused.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Pressure Ulcers","b":"Pressure ulcer codes are combination codes carrying site, laterality and stage together.","p":["L89 codes combine the site, laterality and stage in a single code.","Stage is based on provider documentation; the guidelines permit use of nursing documentation for stage.","Unstageable ulcers and deep tissue pressure injury are distinct concepts with separate codes.","An unstageable ulcer is one whose base is obscured — it is not the same as unspecified stage.","If an ulcer worsens during the admission, assign both the stage on admission and the highest stage reached.","If an ulcer heals completely during the stay, no code is assigned for it at discharge."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Non-Pressure Chronic Ulcers","b":"These require the underlying cause to be sequenced first, which is the opposite habit from pressure ulcers.","p":["L97 and L98 codes require site, laterality and severity.","Severity is described as limited to breakdown of skin, with fat layer exposed, with necrosis of muscle, or with necrosis of bone.","Sequence the underlying cause first — diabetes, atherosclerosis, or venous insufficiency.","The same 'code first' logic applies whether or not the provider explicitly links them, where a 'with' convention exists."]}]},{"n":13,"t":"Diseases of the Musculoskeletal System and Connective Tissue","r":"M00-M99","ref":"I.C.13","i":"The chapter with the most codes. Its central guideline separates traumatic fractures, which belong to Chapter 19, from pathological fractures, which belong here.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Site and Laterality","b":"Most codes require both a site and a side, and some require the bone, joint or muscle affected.","p":["Assign site and laterality wherever the code set provides them.","For conditions affecting multiple sites, use a multiple-site code where one exists.","If no multiple-site code exists, report separate codes for each site."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Acute Traumatic versus Chronic or Recurrent Conditions","b":"The mechanism decides which chapter the code comes from.","p":["Acute traumatic injuries are coded from Chapter 19 using S codes.","Chronic, recurrent, and healed-with-residual conditions are coded from Chapter 13.","If it is unclear, rely on the provider's documentation of the mechanism."]},{"ref":"c","t":"Pathological Fractures","b":"A fracture caused by disease rather than significant trauma is pathological, and it carries its own 7th characters.","p":["A fracture in a patient with osteoporosis and no significant trauma is a PATHOLOGICAL fracture, not traumatic.","Assign M80 for osteoporosis with current pathological fracture.","Use M84.4-, M84.5- or M84.6- for other pathological fractures depending on cause.","7th characters cover initial encounter, subsequent with routine healing, delayed healing, nonunion, malunion and sequela.","Do not assign a traumatic fracture code for a pathological fracture."]}]},{"n":14,"t":"Diseases of the Genitourinary System","r":"N00-N99","ref":"I.C.14","i":"Chapter 14 guidelines centre on chronic kidney disease staging and its mandatory interaction with hypertension coding.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","b":"CKD codes carry a stage, and that stage drives sequencing with hypertension and transplant status.","p":["Assign the N18 code matching the documented stage, N18.1 through N18.5.","End-stage renal disease is N18.6 and requires chronic dialysis.","If both a stage and ESRD are documented, assign N18.6 only.","Add Z99.2 when the patient is on chronic dialysis.","CKD with hypertension is ALWAYS coded with I12 or I13 — the relationship is assumed."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Kidney Transplant Status and Complications","b":"Having a transplant does not by itself mean a complication exists.","p":["Assign Z94.0 for kidney transplant status.","A transplant patient who still has documented CKD is assigned both the CKD stage and Z94.0.","Only assign a transplant complication code when the provider documents a complication of the transplanted organ.","Transplant status alone is never a complication."]}]},{"n":15,"t":"Pregnancy, Childbirth and the Puerperium","r":"O00-O9A","ref":"I.C.15","i":"Chapter 15 codes take absolute sequencing priority on the maternal record, and the trimester character is required on most of them.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"General Rules for Obstetric Cases","b":"Chapter 15 codes are for the maternal record only and always come first.","p":["Chapter 15 codes take sequencing priority over codes from other chapters.","These codes are never used on the newborn record.","Should the provider document that the pregnancy is incidental to the encounter, use Z33.1 instead.","It is the provider's responsibility to state that a condition is not affecting the pregnancy."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Selection of Trimester and Weeks of Gestation","b":"Most Chapter 15 codes carry a trimester character based on the provider's documentation.","p":["Trimester is determined by the provider's documentation of weeks of gestation.","Assign Z3A to identify the specific week of gestation whenever it is documented.","Z3A is not assigned for pregnancy terminations or when weeks are not documented.","If a condition developed before the current admission but is treated during it, use the trimester at the time of the encounter."]},{"ref":"e","t":"Outcome of Delivery","b":"Every delivery episode carries an outcome code on the maternal record.","p":["Assign a code from Z37 on every maternal record where a delivery occurs.","Z37 is never used on the newborn record.","Do not assign Z37 when the delivery occurred prior to admission."]}]},{"n":16,"t":"Certain Conditions Originating in the Perinatal Period","r":"P00-P96","ref":"I.C.16","i":"Chapter 16 codes belong to the newborn record only, and they may persist for life if the condition does.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"General Perinatal Rules","b":"These codes describe conditions originating before or around birth, from the newborn's perspective.","p":["Chapter 16 codes are NEVER used on the maternal record.","A perinatal code may be assigned at any age if the condition originated in the perinatal period.","Code all clinically significant conditions the newborn is evaluated or treated for.","Do not code conditions that are merely observed and require no treatment or evaluation."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Newborn Birth Episode","b":"Every birth admission opens with a place-of-birth code.","p":["Assign a code from Z38 as the principal diagnosis on the newborn birth record.","Z38 is assigned only once, on the birth admission.","Z38 is never used on the maternal record or on a later admission."]}]},{"n":17,"t":"Congenital Malformations, Deformations and Chromosomal Abnormalities","r":"Q00-QA0","ref":"I.C.17","i":"Congenital codes are not limited to infancy and may be assigned across the patient's life.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"General Congenital Rules","b":"A congenital condition is coded for as long as it remains present.","p":["Assign a congenital code at ANY age when the condition is still present.","If the anomaly has been corrected and no longer exists, assign a personal history code instead.","Assign additional codes for any manifestations of the anomaly.","A congenital code may be principal or secondary depending on the encounter."]}]},{"n":18,"t":"Symptoms, Signs and Abnormal Clinical and Laboratory Findings","r":"R00-R99","ref":"I.C.18","i":"Chapter 18 codes are used when no definitive diagnosis has been established, and the rule differs sharply between outpatient and inpatient settings.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Use of Symptom Codes","b":"Whether a symptom is coded depends on whether it is integral to a confirmed diagnosis.","p":["Do NOT code a symptom that is routinely associated with a confirmed diagnosis.","DO code a symptom when it is not routinely associated with the confirmed diagnosis.","When no definitive diagnosis has been established, code the symptom.","Abnormal findings from tests are not coded unless the provider documents their clinical significance."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Uncertain Diagnoses — Setting Matters","b":"This is one of the few places where inpatient and outpatient rules are direct opposites.","p":["OUTPATIENT: never code probable, suspected, questionable, possible or rule-out conditions. Code the symptom instead.","INPATIENT: uncertain diagnoses documented AT DISCHARGE are coded as if they were established.","The inpatient rule applies only to the discharge documentation, not to working diagnoses during the stay."]},{"ref":"c","t":"Coma, Functional Quadriplegia and SIRS","b":"Several R codes have specific additional requirements.","p":["Coma scale codes (R40.2-) may be used with a traumatic brain injury or stroke code, and require the 7th character for when measured.","Functional quadriplegia (R53.2) describes complete immobility due to severe disability, not a spinal injury.","SIRS of non-infectious origin (R65.1-) is used when the provider documents SIRS due to a non-infectious cause."]}]},{"n":19,"t":"Injury, Poisoning and Certain Other Consequences of External Causes","r":"S00-T88","ref":"I.C.19","i":"The largest chapter in the code set. Seventh characters are mandatory on nearly every code, and the poisoning versus adverse effect distinction defines the drug-related guidelines.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Application of 7th Characters","b":"The seventh character records where in the course of care the encounter falls — not how long ago the injury occurred.","p":["A — initial encounter, while the patient is receiving ACTIVE treatment.","D — subsequent encounter, routine care during the healing or recovery phase.","S — sequela, for complications arising as a direct result of a condition.","Fractures use an extended set including B, C, E, F, G, H, J, K, M, N, P, Q, R for open type, healing and union status.","A patient seen by a new provider can still be D — what matters is active treatment, not first contact.","For sequela, code the residual condition first and the injury code with S second."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Coding of Injuries","b":"Injuries are coded individually unless a combination code exists, and sequencing follows severity.","p":["Assign separate codes for each injury unless a combination code is provided.","Sequence the most serious injury first, as determined by the provider.","Superficial injuries such as abrasions or contusions are not coded when associated with a more severe injury of the same site.","When a primary injury results in damage to nerves or blood vessels, sequence the primary injury first."]},{"ref":"d","t":"Coding of Burns and Corrosions","b":"Burns require multiple codes, and students consistently under-report them.","p":["Burns are thermal; corrosions are chemical. They have separate code ranges.","Sequence the code for the highest degree burn first.","Classify by depth, then extent, then external cause.","Assign a code from T31 or T32 for total body surface area when documented.","T31 first digit is total TBSA; second digit is the TBSA that is third degree.","Non-healing burns are coded as acute burns. Necrosis of burned skin is coded as a non-healed burn.","Add the external cause code and the place of occurrence."]},{"ref":"e","t":"Adverse Effects, Poisoning, Underdosing and Toxic Effects","b":"This subsection is the most heavily tested part of the chapter. The distinction rests entirely on whether the drug was correctly prescribed and correctly taken.","p":["ADVERSE EFFECT: drug correctly prescribed and correctly taken. Code the manifestation FIRST, then the T code with 5th or 6th character 5.","POISONING: drug taken incorrectly, overdosed, wrong substance, or combined with alcohol. Code the POISONING FIRST, then the manifestation.","UNDERDOSING: taking less than prescribed. Never a principal or first-listed diagnosis.","For underdosing, code the condition that returned or worsened first, then the underdosing T code.","Add Z91.12- or Z91.13- to explain intentional or unintentional underdosing.","Toxic effects apply to substances that are not drugs, such as household chemicals.","Use the Table of Drugs and Chemicals to find the correct column: accidental, self-harm, assault, undetermined, adverse effect or underdosing."]}]},{"n":20,"t":"External Causes of Morbidity","r":"V00-Y99","ref":"I.C.20","i":"External cause codes describe how an injury happened. They are always secondary and are never sequenced first.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"General External Cause Coding Guidelines","b":"These codes supplement the injury code and may be used with any condition that has an external cause.","p":["An external cause code is NEVER a principal or first-listed diagnosis.","Assign as many external cause codes as necessary to fully explain each cause.","Assign the external cause code for each encounter for which the injury is being treated.","The 7th character on the external cause code must match the 7th character on the injury code."]},{"ref":"b","t":"Place of Occurrence, Activity and Status","b":"These three supplementary codes are reported only once, at the initial encounter.","p":["Y92 records the place of occurrence and is assigned only at the INITIAL encounter.","Y93 records the activity and is also assigned only at the initial encounter.","Y99 records the external cause status — civilian, military, or work-related.","Only one code from each of Y92, Y93 and Y99 is assigned per encounter.","None of these three may be a principal diagnosis."]}]},{"n":21,"t":"Factors Influencing Health Status and Contact with Health Services","r":"Z00-Z99","ref":"I.C.21","i":"Z codes explain encounters where no current disease is being treated, or record a status affecting care. Some may be first-listed, others only secondary.","s":[{"ref":"c.4","t":"History (of)","b":"Personal and family history codes explain risk and influence future care.","p":["Personal history means the condition is resolved but may recur or affect treatment.","Family history means the patient is at increased risk.","History codes may be used with follow-up codes and screening codes to explain the reason for the encounter."]},{"ref":"c.5","t":"Screening","b":"Screening means testing an apparently well patient. The presence of a symptom changes everything.","p":["Screening applies when the patient has NO signs or symptoms of the condition.","If a sign or symptom prompted the test, code the sign or symptom instead — it is diagnostic, not screening.","A screening code may be first-listed when screening is the reason for the encounter.","Assign the screening code additionally if the screening is done during an encounter for another reason."]},{"ref":"c.7","t":"Aftercare","b":"Aftercare is care during healing, not treatment of the original problem — and it never applies to injuries.","p":["Aftercare codes cover situations where the initial treatment is complete and the patient needs continued care during healing.","Aftercare codes are NOT used for injuries. Use the injury code with 7th character D instead.","Aftercare codes may be first-listed when the encounter is solely for aftercare.","Do not use an aftercare code with a diagnosis code for the active condition being treated."]},{"ref":"c.15","t":"Observation","b":"Observation codes are narrow, and using them when another explanation exists is an error.","p":["Observation codes are used only when a suspected condition is ruled out.","If any sign, symptom or diagnosis explains the encounter, code that instead.","Observation codes may only be principal, not secondary — with limited exceptions."]}]},{"n":22,"t":"Codes for Special Purposes","r":"U00-U85","ref":"I.C.22","i":"A small chapter reserved for provisional assignment of new conditions and for emergency use.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Use of Special Purpose Codes","b":"This chapter changes between updates more than any other.","p":["Used for newly identified conditions of uncertain aetiology and for emergency situations.","Always check current CMS guidance before assigning, as codes here are added and retired between annual updates."]}]}]},"cpt":{"label":"CPT","sub":"Section guidelines","color":"#00A88E","chapters":[{"n":"Evaluation and Management","r":"99202-99499","ref":"E/M","i":"The E/M guidelines define who counts as a new patient, how levels are selected, and what may be counted toward medical decision making or time.","s":[{"ref":"1","t":"New and Established Patients","b":"The distinction rests on prior face-to-face service within a defined period, judged at the group and specialty level rather than the individual physician.","p":["A new patient has not received a professional face-to-face service from the physician, or another physician of the same specialty and subspecialty in the same group, within the past three years.","An established patient has received such a service within three years.","Emergency department services make no new versus established distinction.","When a physician is on call for another, classify the patient as if by the physician who is unavailable."]},{"ref":"2","t":"Levels of Service — Medical Decision Making","b":"MDM is assessed on three elements, and the level is reached when two of the three are met or exceeded.","p":["The three elements are: number and complexity of problems addressed; amount and complexity of data reviewed and analysed; and risk of complications, morbidity or mortality.","Two of the three elements must be met or exceeded to qualify for a given level.","History and examination are performed as clinically appropriate but no longer determine the level.","Problems addressed means those evaluated or treated at the encounter, including those where treatment is deliberately deferred.","Do not count the same data element twice across categories."]},{"ref":"3","t":"Levels of Service — Time","b":"Time may be used instead of MDM for most office and outpatient services, and it means total time on the date of the encounter.","p":["Total time includes both face-to-face and non-face-to-face work performed by the physician on the date of the encounter.","Counted activities include reviewing records, ordering tests, counselling, and documenting in the record.","Time spent by clinical staff does not count toward the physician's time.","Time on a different calendar day is not counted.","When time is used for level selection, the total time must be documented."]},{"ref":"4","t":"Reporting E/M with Procedures","b":"An E/M performed on the same day as a procedure is only separately reportable when it goes beyond the usual work of that procedure.","p":["Append modifier 25 when a significant, separately identifiable E/M is performed on the same day as a procedure.","The E/M must exceed the usual pre- and post-operative work associated with the procedure.","The decision to perform a minor procedure is included in that procedure and is not separately reportable.","The decision for major surgery may be reported with modifier 57."]}]},{"n":"Anesthesia","r":"00100-01999","ref":"Anesthesia","i":"The anaesthesia guidelines cover what the service includes, how time is measured, and the modifiers describing patient condition and circumstances.","s":[{"ref":"1","t":"Time Reporting","b":"Anaesthesia time is a continuous measured period with clearly defined start and end points.","p":["Time begins when the anaesthetist starts preparing the patient for induction.","Time ends when the anaesthetist is no longer in personal attendance.","Time is reported in minutes and converted to units according to payer rules.","Discontinuous time periods may be added together."]},{"ref":"2","t":"Physical Status Modifiers","b":"These describe the patient's condition at the time of anaesthesia and may affect payment.","p":["P1 — a normal healthy patient.","P2 — a patient with mild systemic disease.","P3 — a patient with severe systemic disease.","P4 — a patient with severe systemic disease that is a constant threat to life.","P5 — a moribund patient not expected to survive without the operation.","P6 — a declared brain-dead patient whose organs are being removed for donor purposes."]},{"ref":"3","t":"Qualifying Circumstances and Multiple Procedures","b":"Added complexity is reported separately, and only one anaesthesia code applies per session.","p":["Qualifying circumstances codes report anaesthesia complicated by extreme age, hypothermia, controlled hypotension or emergency conditions.","When multiple surgical procedures are performed under one anaesthetic, report only the code with the highest base unit value.","Total time for all procedures is combined and reported with that single code."]}]},{"n":"Surgery — General and the Surgical Package","r":"10004-69990","ref":"Surgery","i":"The surgical package definition determines what is bundled into a procedure code and what may be reported separately.","s":[{"ref":"1","t":"The Surgical Package Definition","b":"A CPT surgical code covers a defined set of services beyond the operation itself.","p":["Included: the operation itself.","Included: local infiltration, metacarpal or digital block, or topical anaesthesia.","Included: the evaluation and management encounter on the date immediately prior to or on the date of the procedure.","Included: immediate postoperative care, writing orders, and evaluating the patient in the recovery area.","Included: typical follow-up care.","NOT included: complications requiring a return to the operating room.","NOT included: treatment of the underlying condition unrelated to recovery.","NOT included: the initial consultation or evaluation at which the decision for surgery was made."]},{"ref":"2","t":"Separate Procedures","b":"Codes designated as separate procedures are components of larger services and are only reported when performed alone.","p":["A code marked 'separate procedure' is not reported when performed as an integral part of a larger service.","It may be reported when performed independently or is unrelated to the other services provided.","When reported independently, append modifier 59 or a more specific X modifier."]},{"ref":"3","t":"Add-on Codes and Modifier 51 Exempt Codes","b":"Add-on codes describe additional work and cannot stand alone.","p":["Add-on codes are marked with a + and are always performed in addition to a primary procedure.","Never report an add-on code alone.","Add-on codes are exempt from modifier 51 — do not append it.","Some codes are designated modifier 51 exempt and are marked accordingly."]}]},{"n":"Surgery — Integumentary System","r":"10004-19499","ref":"Integumentary","i":"Lesion excision, wound repair and grafting. Measurement method decides the code, and it is where most integumentary errors originate.","s":[{"ref":"1","t":"Excision of Lesions","b":"The measurement is taken before excision and includes the margins, not just the lesion.","p":["Measure the greatest clinical diameter of the lesion PLUS the narrowest margin required, on both sides.","Measure before excision — tissue shrinks once placed in formalin.","Benign and malignant lesions are reported from separate code ranges; the pathology report determines which.","Each lesion excised is reported separately.","The closure is included if it is a simple repair. Intermediate and complex repairs are reported additionally."]},{"ref":"2","t":"Repair (Closure)","b":"Repairs are classified by complexity, and lengths are added together only within matching classifications and anatomic groups.","p":["Simple repair — superficial wound requiring one-layer closure.","Intermediate repair — layered closure, or single-layer closure of a heavily contaminated wound requiring extensive cleaning.","Complex repair — more than layered closure, such as scar revision, debridement, or extensive undermining.","Add together the lengths of repairs in the SAME classification and SAME anatomic group, and report one code.","Do not add together repairs of different classifications or different anatomic groups.","Debridement is reported separately only when gross contamination requires prolonged cleaning or when appreciable amounts of devitalised tissue are removed."]},{"ref":"3","t":"Skin Grafts and Flaps","b":"Graft codes are based on the recipient site, not the donor site.","p":["Report the size of the RECIPIENT site.","The donor site repair is reported separately only when it requires a graft or complex closure.","Grafts are classified by type: split-thickness, full-thickness, pinch, or tissue-cultured.","Surgical preparation of the recipient site may be separately reportable."]}]},{"n":"Surgery — Musculoskeletal System","r":"20100-29999","ref":"Musculoskeletal","i":"Fracture care and arthroscopy. The terms open and closed do not describe the fracture itself but the method of treatment.","s":[{"ref":"1","t":"Fracture and Dislocation Treatment","b":"Treatment type refers to the surgical approach, not to whether the fracture broke the skin.","p":["CLOSED treatment means the fracture site is not surgically opened — it may still involve manipulation.","OPEN treatment means the fracture site is surgically opened and visualised, or opened remotely for nailing.","PERCUTANEOUS skeletal fixation is neither open nor closed — fixation is placed across the fracture under imaging.","Manipulation means the fracture is reduced or restored to alignment.","The application of the first cast or traction device is included in the fracture care code.","Replacement casts during the follow-up period are reported separately."]},{"ref":"2","t":"Arthroscopy","b":"A diagnostic scope is bundled into a surgical scope of the same joint.","p":["Diagnostic arthroscopy is included when a surgical arthroscopy is performed on the same joint.","When an arthroscopic procedure is converted to an open procedure, report only the open procedure.","Report each distinct compartment procedure where the codes allow it, subject to payer edits."]}]},{"n":"Radiology","r":"70010-79999","ref":"Radiology","i":"Radiology guidelines govern component billing, what counts as contrast, and the written report requirement.","s":[{"ref":"1","t":"Professional and Technical Components","b":"Diagnostic imaging has two parts, and ownership of the equipment decides which is billed.","p":["Modifier 26 reports the PROFESSIONAL component — the physician's interpretation and written report only.","Modifier TC reports the TECHNICAL component — equipment, supplies and technologist, with no interpretation.","No modifier reports the GLOBAL service, where the same entity provides both parts.","Never report 26 and TC on the same line for the same provider.","A written, signed interpretation is required to bill the professional component."]},{"ref":"2","t":"Administration of Contrast","b":"The route of administration determines whether a study qualifies as 'with contrast'.","p":["'With contrast' means contrast administered intravascularly, intra-articularly or intrathecally.","Oral or rectal contrast alone does NOT qualify as 'with contrast'.","'Without and with contrast' requires images obtained both before and after contrast administration.","The contrast material itself may be separately reportable with a HCPCS code."]},{"ref":"3","t":"Views and Documentation","b":"The number of views is part of the code descriptor and must be supported by the report.","p":["Count the views documented in the report and select the code that matches.","If fewer views are taken than the code describes, select the lower code or append a modifier as payer rules require.","The report must document the findings and the interpretation."]}]},{"n":"Pathology and Laboratory","r":"80047-89398","ref":"Pathology","i":"Guidelines here cover panel composition and the levelling of surgical pathology by specimen.","s":[{"ref":"1","t":"Organ or Disease Oriented Panels","b":"Panel codes require every listed component. Missing one changes the coding entirely.","p":["A panel code may only be reported when ALL listed component tests are performed.","If one component is missing, report the individual tests instead of the panel.","Do not report a panel and its component tests separately.","Tests performed in addition to those in a panel are reported separately."]},{"ref":"2","t":"Surgical Pathology Levels","b":"Levelling is by specimen complexity, and the specimen — not the block or slide — is the unit of service.","p":["The unit of service is the SPECIMEN: each separately identified and separately labelled container.","Two containers of the same tissue type are two units of service.","Levels 88302 through 88309 reflect increasing specimen complexity, not the number of blocks or slides.","88300 is used for a specimen requiring gross examination only.","Special stains, immunohistochemistry and decalcification are reported additionally."]}]},{"n":"Medicine","r":"90281-99607","ref":"Medicine","i":"A broad section. The recurring rule is that a product and its administration are almost always separate codes.","s":[{"ref":"1","t":"Immunisations","b":"Two codes are always required: one for the vaccine product and one for administering it.","p":["Report the vaccine or toxoid product code AND the administration code.","Administration codes differ by route and by whether counselling was provided.","Counselling administration codes apply to patients through 18 years of age.","When an E/M is also performed and is significant and separately identifiable, append modifier 25 to the E/M."]},{"ref":"2","t":"Cardiovascular Diagnostic Services","b":"Many studies split into tracing, interpretation and report — mirroring the radiology component logic.","p":["93000 reports the global ECG service: tracing, interpretation and report.","93005 reports the tracing only, without interpretation.","93010 reports the interpretation and report only.","Select based on which part of the service your provider performed."]}]}]},"pcs":{"label":"ICD-10-PCS","sub":"Section guidelines","color":"#8B5CF6","chapters":[{"n":"Character structure","t":"Character structure","r":"7 characters","ref":"7 characters","i":"Every PCS code is exactly seven characters and each position always carries the same meaning. Codes are built from tables, never looked up whole.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"The seven positions","b":"","p":["1 — Section. Medical and Surgical is 0.","2 — Body system.","3 — Root operation.","4 — Body part.","5 — Approach.","6 — Device left in place.","7 — Qualifier.","Valid values are 0-9 and A-H, J-N, P-Z. The letters I and O are never used, to avoid confusion with 1 and 0.","If a character has no applicable value, use Z. Never leave it blank."]}]},{"n":"Root operations — taking out","t":"Root operations — taking out","r":"Character 3","ref":"Character 3","i":"The root operation states the objective of the procedure. These are the ones concerned with removing tissue or matter.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Definitions","b":"","p":["Excision — cutting out SOME of a body part.","Resection — cutting out ALL of a body part.","Detachment — cutting off all or part of an extremity.","Destruction — eradicating a body part by energy, force or a destructive agent.","Extraction — pulling or stripping out by force.","Drainage — taking out fluids or gases.","Extirpation — taking out solid matter such as a stone or clot.","Fragmentation — breaking solid matter into pieces."]}]},{"n":"Root operations — other groups","t":"Root operations — other groups","r":"Character 3","ref":"Character 3","i":"The remaining root operations cover moving, altering, device work and diagnostic procedures.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Definitions","b":"","p":["Bypass — altering the route of passage of contents.","Dilation — expanding an orifice or lumen.","Occlusion — completely closing an orifice or lumen.","Restriction — partially closing an orifice or lumen.","Reposition — moving a body part to its normal location.","Transfer — moving a body part to function for a similar part, keeping its blood supply.","Transplantation — putting in a living body part from a person or animal.","Replacement — putting in material that takes over the function of a body part.","Supplement — putting in material that reinforces or augments a body part.","Insertion — putting in a device that does not do any of the above.","Removal — taking out a device. Revision — correcting a malfunctioning device.","Inspection — visual or manual exploration only."]}]},{"n":"Approach","t":"Approach","r":"Character 5","ref":"Character 5","i":"The approach describes the technique used to reach the operative site. There are seven.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"The seven approaches","b":"","p":["Open — cutting through skin or membrane to expose the site.","Percutaneous — puncture or minor incision to reach the site.","Percutaneous endoscopic — puncture plus visualisation.","Via natural or artificial opening — through an existing orifice.","Via natural or artificial opening endoscopic — with visualisation.","Via natural or artificial opening with percutaneous endoscopic assistance.","External — performed on the surface or indirectly through the skin."]}]},{"n":"Building and sequencing","t":"Building and sequencing","r":"Conventions","ref":"Conventions","i":"Rules that resolve most of the situations that confuse new inpatient coders.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Conventions","b":"","p":["All seven values must come from a SINGLE ROW of one table. Never mix values across rows.","The Index points you to a table, not to a finished code.","Code the root operation the surgeon actually performed, not the one named in the operative title.","If multiple root operations are performed, code each one separately.","A procedure discontinued before completion is coded to the root operation actually performed — often Inspection.","Biopsy is Excision, Extraction or Drainage with qualifier X for diagnostic.","A biopsy followed by more definitive treatment at the same session: code both.","Converting from laparoscopic to open codes the open approach only."]}]}]},"hcpcs":{"label":"HCPCS Level II","sub":"Code family guidelines","color":"#E5A82E","chapters":[{"n":"What HCPCS Level II covers","t":"What HCPCS Level II covers","r":"A-V","ref":"A-V","i":"HCPCS Level II covers products, supplies, drugs and services that CPT does not. Codes are one letter followed by four digits, maintained by CMS.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Scope","b":"","p":["Maintained by CMS, not the AMA.","Required by Medicare and Medicaid, and increasingly used by commercial payers.","CPT is sometimes referred to as HCPCS Level I."]}]},{"n":"Code families by letter","t":"Code families by letter","r":"A-V","ref":"A-V","i":"The opening letter groups the code by what it describes. Knowing the families speeds up lookup considerably.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"The families","b":"","p":["A — ambulance, medical and surgical supplies, dressings.","B — enteral and parenteral therapy.","C — outpatient PPS pass-through items.","E — durable medical equipment.","G — temporary procedures and professional services for Medicare.","J — drugs administered other than by mouth, plus chemotherapy drugs.","K — temporary DME codes.","L — orthotics and prosthetics.","P — pathology and laboratory services.","Q — temporary codes.","R — diagnostic radiology services.","V — vision and hearing services."]}]},{"n":"J codes and drug units","t":"J codes and drug units","r":"J0000-J9999","ref":"J0000-J9999","i":"J codes are where most HCPCS errors happen, because each code defines a specific quantity and the units billed must match the dose documented.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Unit calculation","b":"","p":["Read the descriptor for the unit — it may be per mg, per 10 mg, per 50 mg or per vial.","Calculate units from the documented dose administered, not from the vial size.","Wastage may be separately billable when documented, subject to payer rules.","Always report the administration CPT code alongside the drug code.","Route matters — J codes are for drugs given other than by mouth."]}]},{"n":"DME modifiers","t":"DME modifiers","r":"E, K codes","ref":"E, K codes","i":"Durable medical equipment relies on modifiers to describe rental, purchase and medical necessity.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Common modifiers","b":"","p":["NU — new equipment purchase.","RR — rental.","UE — used equipment purchase.","KX — documentation on file supports medical necessity.","GA — waiver of liability on file, an ABN was signed.","GY — item statutorily excluded, not a Medicare benefit.","GZ — expected to be denied, no ABN on file.","Left and right are identified with LT and RT."]}]},{"n":"Choosing HCPCS over CPT","t":"Choosing HCPCS over CPT","r":"General","ref":"General","i":"When both a CPT and a HCPCS code appear to describe the service, payer instruction decides which to report.","s":[{"ref":"a","t":"Selection","b":"","p":["Medicare often requires a G code where CPT has an apparent equivalent.","Check the payer policy before defaulting to CPT.","Never report both codes for the same service."]}]}]}};
